{
  "slug": "artist:warren-g",
  "name": "Warren G",
  "editorial_article": "Born Warren Griffin III on November 10, 1970, in Long Beach, California, the artist became a pivotal architect of G-funk, a subgenre he helped propel into the global mainstream during the 1990s. After honing his skills in the back of the V.I.P. Records store with his childhood trio 213—comprising Snoop Dogg and Nate Dogg—Griffin launched a successful solo career with his 1994 Def Jam debut, Regulate... G Funk Era. The title track, a duet with Nate Dogg that samples Michael McDonald's \"I Keep Forgettin' (Every Time You're Near),\" reached number two on the Billboard Hot 100 and spent 18 weeks in the Top 40. Known for a smoother, more melodic approach compared to his gangsta-rap contemporaries, he continued to collaborate with artists like Ron Isley and Adina Howard on subsequent projects such as 1997's Take a Look Over Your Shoulder. In 2015, he returned to his roots with the EP Regulate... G Funk Era, Part II, featuring archived vocals from his late collaborator Nate Dogg.",
